PrepFlow
Admin CMS Documentation

Version: v1.0

Status: Draft

Author: Tejas Nere

1. Purpose

The Admin Content Management System (CMS) is designed to allow administrators to manage the complete PrepFlow platform without modifying source code.

Every content update should happen through the Admin CMS.

The administrator should never need to edit MongoDB documents manually or redeploy the application.

2. Vision

The Admin CMS should feel like using Notion instead of a database management tool.

The focus should be on productivity, simplicity, and flexibility.

3. Primary Objective

Allow administrators to manage every aspect of the platform without writing code.

Examples include:

Adding Subjects
Creating Chapters
Uploading PDFs
Managing Resources
Creating Tags
Managing Metadata
Publishing Content

Everything should happen through the dashboard.

4. CMS Philosophy

The administrator manages content.

The developer manages features.

Content updates should never require source code modifications.

5. Authentication

Only authenticated administrators can access the CMS.

Students should never access admin pages.

Admin routes should remain hidden from public navigation.

6. Dashboard

The dashboard should display:

Total Subjects
Total Chapters
Total Resources
Published Resources
Draft Resources
Recently Updated Content

The dashboard should provide a quick overview of the platform.

7. Subject Management

The administrator should be able to:

Create Subject
Edit Subject
Delete Subject
Hide Subject
Publish Subject
Change Display Order
8. Chapter Management

The administrator should be able to:

Create Chapter
Edit Chapter
Delete Chapter
Hide Chapter
Publish Chapter
Reorder Chapters
Assign Subject
9. Resource Management

Each chapter can contain multiple resources.

The administrator should be able to:

Add Resource
Edit Resource
Delete Resource
Hide Resource
Publish Resource
Reorder Resources
10. Supported Resource Types

Initially:

Playlist
Notes
Formula Sheet
Previous Year Questions

Future versions may include:

Mock Tests
Flashcards
Mind Maps
Assignments
Books
Quiz

The system should support new resource types without frontend code changes.

11. File Upload

The administrator uploads files directly through the CMS.

Supported files:

PDF
Images

Files should automatically upload to Supabase Storage.

The generated URL should automatically be stored inside MongoDB.

The administrator should never manually copy storage URLs.

12. Metadata Management

Every chapter and resource supports optional metadata.

Examples:

Estimated Study Time

Difficulty

Teacher

Importance

Language

Duration

Metadata is completely optional.

13. Dynamic Metadata

The administrator should be able to create custom metadata fields.

Example:

Field Name

Batch

Value

2027

Another example:

Field Name

Expected Questions

Value

2-3

The frontend should automatically display newly created metadata.

No code changes should be required.

14. Tags Management

Administrators should create unlimited tags.

Examples:

High Weightage
Beginner Friendly
Important
Conceptual
Revision

Tags should be reusable across chapters and resources.

15. Visibility

Every item should support visibility.

Available states:

Draft

Published

Hidden

Students should only receive published content.

16. Ordering

Administrators should change ordering without writing code.

Supported ordering:

Subjects

Chapters

Resources

Ordering methods:

Drag & Drop

Move Up

Move Down

17. Search

The CMS should support searching by:

Subject

Chapter

Resource

Tag

Teacher

Metadata

18. Filters

The administrator should filter content by:

Subject

Status

Resource Type

Tags

Teacher

Publication Status

19. Preview Mode

The administrator should preview content exactly as students see it.

Preview mode should never expose editing controls.

20. Auto Save

The CMS should automatically save changes whenever possible.

Manual save should remain available.

21. Version History

Every content update should create a version.

The administrator should be able to:

View History

Restore Previous Version

Track Changes

22. Audit Log

Every modification should store:

Created By

Updated By

Created At

Updated At

This improves accountability and debugging.

23. Soft Delete

Deleting content should not permanently remove it.

Deleted content should move to Trash.

The administrator can restore deleted items later.

24. Bulk Operations

The CMS should support:

Bulk Delete

Bulk Publish

Bulk Hide

Bulk Move

Bulk Tag Assignment

Future:

CSV Import

JSON Import

25. Future Integrations

The CMS should later support:

Battle Questions

Mock Tests

AI Recommendations

Announcements

Planner Content

Community Management

without architectural changes.

26. UI Principles

The CMS should feel:

Fast

Simple

Professional

Organized

Minimal

Every action should require the fewest possible clicks.

27. Golden Rules
Rule 1

No administrator should edit source code for content updates.

Rule 2

No administrator should manually edit MongoDB collections.

Rule 3

No administrator should manually upload storage URLs.

Rule 4

Everything should be editable.

Rule 5

Everything should be configurable.

Rule 6

Everything should be dynamic.

Rule 7

Everything should be reusable.

Rule 8

The CMS should remain scalable.

28. Future Enhancements

Future versions may include:

Approval Workflow

Multiple Administrators

Role-Based Permissions

Content Scheduling

Automatic Resource Validation

AI-assisted Content Entry

Analytics Dashboard

29. Final Statement

The Admin CMS is the backbone of PrepFlow.

A successful CMS ensures that platform growth happens through content management instead of software development.

The administrator should focus on improving student learning rather than maintaining application code.